3. Formal Definitions and Operationalization

3.1 Three-level latent variable architecture

A persistent problem in v0.2 was unit commensurability: comparing "frontier compute (FLOP/s)" to "nuclear yield equivalents" to "energy density" in a single power index  P(t)  assumes an equivalence that does not exist at the measurement level. This is resolved by a three-level architecture.

Level 1 — Raw indicators.  X_i^{raw} : directly measured quantities in their native units. Examples: training FLOP for frontier models; number of actors with nuclear weapons; DNA synthesis cost per base pair; share of cyber-physical actions without human-in-loop.

Level 2 — Normalized indices.  X_i^{norm} \in [0,1]  or standardized z-scores: each raw indicator is normalized against a reference period or theoretical maximum. This step does not assume cross-indicator comparability; it only makes each indicator dimensionless and bounded.

Level 3 — Latent constructs.  X^{latent} : the theoretically relevant variable, estimated as a weighted composite of normalized indicators via factor analysis, principal components, or confirmatory factor analysis.

For example, technological power is not directly observed. It is a latent construct:

P^{latent}(t) = f(P^{compute}, P^{bio}, P^{nuclear}, P^{industrial}, \ldots; \mathbf{w}_P)

where  \mathbf{w}_P  is a vector of factor loadings estimated from data. The estimation procedure must satisfy two constraints: (i) indicators within a construct should exhibit sufficient inter-correlation to justify a common factor (testable via Cronbach's  \alpha  or factor loadings); (ii) indicators across constructs should not be so highly correlated as to render the constructs indistinguishable (testable via discriminant validity).

Implication for the model: All variables in the operational model are latent constructs estimated from multiple indicators. This directly addresses the criticism that  P  and  A  are not commensurable: they are not compared directly; they are each constructed from their own indicator sets, and the model estimates the relationship between the latent constructs.

3.2 Operationalizing  G(t) : the effective governance construct

The v0.2 treatment of governance — "number of frameworks adopted" — was correctly identified as insufficient. Framework adoption is not framework effectiveness. The 2026 International AI Safety Report notes that frontier AI capabilities are advancing rapidly while safeguards are improving but not fast enough, with an evaluation gap between laboratory benchmark performance and real-world deployment reliability. Framework adoption counts alone would measure safety theater rather than safety capacity.

The effective governance construct is decomposed multiplicatively:

G_{\text{eff}}(t) = G_{\text{adoption}}(t) \times G_{\text{coverage}}(t) \times G_{\text{enforcement}}(t) \times G_{\text{compliance}}(t)

each component normalized to  [0,1] .

Operationalization for the AI-safety domain:

·  G_{\text{adoption}} : fraction of frontier developers with a published safety framework. As of early 2026, this includes Anthropic (RSP v3.0, February 2026), OpenAI (Preparedness Framework v2, April 2025), and Google DeepMind (FSF v3.1, April 2026). NIST's CAISI expanded its frontier model evaluation program to five major labs — Google DeepMind, Microsoft, and xAI signed pre-deployment testing agreements on May 5, 2026, joining OpenAI and Anthropic.
·  G_{\text{coverage}} : fraction of critical capability classes (e.g., CBRN, cyber, autonomous replication) covered by framework commitments. Google DeepMind's FSF v3.1 added Tracked Capability Levels, an earlier-warning layer intended to flag less extreme risks before a Critical Capability Level is reached, and gave more detail on holistic risk assessments. This is an increase in coverage, but the fraction of all relevant capability classes covered remains an empirical question.
·  G_{\text{enforcement}} : strength of legal or regulatory mechanisms. California's SB 53 (Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act) took effect January 1, 2026, imposing transparency and reporting requirements on covered developers, with some provisions effective January 1, 2027. New York's RAISE Act was signed into law on March 27, 2026, with an effective date of January 1, 2027. The distinction between in force and signed, pending is itself a measurable component of enforcement strength: a law that takes effect in 2027 provides no enforcement in 2026.
·  G_{\text{compliance}} : observable adherence. This is the hardest to measure and most subject to Goodhart's law. Proposed proxies: incident reporting rates (SB 53 requires safety incident reporting); third-party audit findings; and the gap between stated framework commitments and observed deployment practices. The 2026 Safety Index reports that no company scored higher than a C– on existential safety criteria, with the industry leader receiving a D+, and that lab commitments to pause if redlines are approached have been "weakened or voided" — described by experts as "moving goalposts". This suggests  G_{\text{compliance}}  is substantially below  G_{\text{adoption}} .

Limitation: This decomposition does not solve the incentive-compatibility problem. If the actors subject to governance are also the actors who fund and shape the governance mechanisms,  G_{\text{enforcement}}  and  G_{\text{compliance}}  may be performative. The model should be tested with an explicit variable for governance independence, provisionally defined as the fraction of governance funding and personnel not derived from the regulated industry.

3.3 Operationalizing  V(t)  and  C(t) 

Verification  V(t) :

·  V_{\text{adoption}} : fraction of framework claims subject to independent audit.
·  V_{\text{replication}} : rate at which key empirical claims in the relevant domain are independently replicated. In AI safety, this is currently near zero for frontier model evaluations. In climate science, the ozone case provides a benchmark: the Molina–Rowland hypothesis was independently verified through atmospheric chemistry replication before policy action.
·  V_{\text{adversarial}} : frequency and quality of adversarial red-teaming, including independent efforts. The 2026 International AI Safety Report notes that real-world evidence for several risks is growing and that layering multiple approaches is necessary.

Correction  C(t) :

·  C_{\text{detection}} : mean time from capability emergence to detection.
·  C_{\text{response}} : mean time from detection to corrective action (policy change, deployment pause, technical mitigation).
·  C_{\text{memory}} : institutional persistence of corrective measures. A correction that is reversed within two years is not a correction in the relevant sense.

The Montreal Protocol provides a calibration anchor: approximately 10 years from observation (1980s ozone depletion detection) to correction (1987 Protocol), with sustained institutional memory through the Protocol's ongoing monitoring framework. COVID-19 provides a contrast: approximately 2 years from detection to vaccine deployment, but with significant institutional memory degradation in the years following. The AI governance domain currently shows a lag exceeding three years from the release of ChatGPT (November 2022) to the first binding regulatory requirements (SB 53, January 2026), with no binding international treaty in force.

