7. AI as a Double-Edged Variable

AI appears on both sides of the correction condition. This is modeled explicitly:

\text{AI}(t) = \text{AI}_{\text{risk}}(t) + \text{AI}_{\text{correction}}(t)

where  \text{AI}_{\text{risk}}  contributes to  P  and  A , and  \text{AI}_{\text{correction}}  contributes to  G ,  V ,  C , and  R . The net effect is positive only when correction use is open, verifiable, and educational. The design constraint from v0.1 is retained:

\boxed{\text{AI should increase civilization's ability to think, not eliminate civilization's ability to think.}}

Adversarial consideration: can  G  itself increase  K ? This is Manheim's (2020) central point and must be modeled explicitly. Global AI safety standards that mandate a common safety library for all labs, or a common evaluation framework, create a shared software substrate that functions as a common-cause failure channel. If all frontier labs use the same safety classifier, a single vulnerability in that classifier affects all labs simultaneously. This is a case where increasing  G  (governance coverage) increases  K  (coupling), potentially reducing  R_{\text{eff}} . The model must therefore include a term for the coupling generated by governance mechanisms themselves:

K_{\text{total}}(t) = K_{\text{technical}}(t) + K_{\text{governance}}(t)

where  K_{\text{governance}}  is the coupling introduced by shared regulatory or safety infrastructure. The design implication is that governance mechanisms should prioritize interoperability without monoculture — shared standards that allow independent implementations, not shared implementations that create single points of failure.

NASA–IBM Lunar Foundation Model (September 2026): This illustrates both sides. It is an open-source tool that supports mapping and resource assessment for sustained human presence, demonstrating AI's corrective potential. But if it depends on Earth-based foundation models and compute for updates and inference, it does not increase S-autonomy in the existential sense.

