8. Multiplanetary Correction Principle and Autonomy Ladder

8.1 The principle

A civilization becomes more robust to planet-specific existential hazards as sufficiently independent habitats increase in number, diversity, and autonomy, provided their interdependence does not create a new common-mode failure.

8.2 Autonomy Ladder derived from failure-mode analysis

The v0.2 ladder used intuitive criteria. The v0.3 ladder is derived from an explicit failure-mode analysis: each level specifies which dependencies must be severed to protect against which class of existential risk.

Level Designation Criterion Failure modes mitigated Existential-risk reduction
S0 Dependent outpost Life support, energy, software, and comms all Earth-dependent None None
S1 Resource-assisted ISRU demonstrated for propellant, water, or oxygen Partial: reduces resupply dependence but not informational or industrial dependence Negligible
S2 Materially semi-closed ≥90% mass closure for critical consumables Partial: reduces consumable dependence but not software, replacement parts, or governance Modest
S3 Informationally independent Can fork and maintain critical software without Earth; has local Git mirror of life-critical code; can train or operate a small fallback model locally Informational: protects against Earth-based software failure, cyberattack on Earth infrastructure, or communication loss Hypothesized significant, conditional on  K 
S4 Politically survival-autonomous Can make survival-critical decisions without Earth approval within light-delay constraints; has independent governance for emergency response Political: protects against Earth-based governance failure, geopolitical conflict, or policy capture Hypothesized full, conditional on  K 

The S3 claim is a hypothesis, not a result. A habitat at S3 that depends on Earth for replacement parts, energy components, or biological supplies is not existentially redundant. The condition is:

S_3 + R_{\text{industrial}} + R_{\text{energy}} + R_{\text{biological}} + R_{\text{informational}} \rightarrow \text{potential redundancy}

Not  S_3 \Rightarrow \text{survival} .

8.3 Coupling condition

The effective redundancy of the multiplanetary system is:

R_{\text{multiplanetary}} = \sum_i \frac{R_i}{1 + \lambda K_i}

where  K_i  is the coupling of node  i  to Earth and to other nodes. If  K_i \approx 1  for all  i , multiplanetary expansion adds no resilience. The beta-factor derivation of  \lambda  applies:  \lambda = \beta/(1-\beta) , where  \beta  is the common-cause failure fraction for the multiplanetary system.

Falsifier: If multiplanetary nodes remain at S0–S2 for more than two decades after establishment, they should be treated as coupled colonies, not redundancy, and should not be counted toward existential-risk reduction.

