9. Historical Calibration: Comparative Case Design

Phase II begins with the Montreal Protocol / ozone recovery as the primary positive case and uses a comparative design to test whether the correction loop model explains both successes and failures.

9.1 Montreal Protocol (primary positive case)

Documented sequence:

Stage Event Approximate date
Observation Ozone depletion detected 1980s
Detection Antarctic ozone hole confirmed 1985
Hypothesis CFCs as causal agent (Molina–Rowland) 1974
Testing Atmospheric chemistry replication 1970s–1980s
Evaluation Scientific consensus and policy assessment 1985–1987
Correction Montreal Protocol signed 1987
Memory Ongoing monitoring under the Protocol 1987–present
New observation Ozone layer on track for recovery 2020s

The Protocol phased out >99% of controlled ozone-depleting substances. Nature reported in June 2024 that harm from some gases is starting to fall and the hole could close sooner than expected, with statistical confirmation in Nature (March 2025) that recovery is due to reduction of ODS.

9.2 Comparative design: ozone vs. other planetary boundaries

The Planetary Health Check 2025 provides a natural comparative experiment. Seven of nine boundaries are breached; only ozone and aerosol loading remain safe. The ozone case is the one where the full correction loop — observation → causal discovery → verification → international agreement → implementation → monitoring → correction — operated successfully. The other boundaries are cases where the loop is incomplete, delayed, or captured.

Research design: Compare the loop variables (detection lag, verification strength, governance adoption, compliance) across domains. The prediction is that domains with stronger loop variables show measurable correction; domains with weaker loop variables do not.

This does not prove CLCA. It tests whether the loop model has explanatory power across domains.

9.3 Secondary cases

· Cuban Missile Crisis (1962): high  D , low  V , recovery via  C  (crisis communication and back-channel diplomacy). Illustrates correction without prior verification infrastructure.
· COVID-19 (2020–2023): early detection failure, later  V + C + R . Illustrates the cost of detection lag.
· AI governance lag (2023–2026): rise in  P, A  while  G, V  lag. Illustrates the condition in which  \Gamma < 1 .

