10. Falsifiers (Strengthened)

The theory should make itself vulnerable to quantitative evidence:

1. Detection-lag falsifier: If time to detect frontier capability jumps exceeds time to deploy globally, then  V  is insufficient regardless of  G .
2. Coupling falsifier: If  R  increases but recovery time after shocks also increases, then redundancy is coupled and  R_{\text{eff}}  is not growing.
3. Autonomy falsifier: If multiplanetary nodes remain at S0–S2 for more than two decades after establishment, then  S  contributes negligibly to existential-risk reduction.
4. Framework-adoption falsifier: If safety-framework adoption count grows while the evaluation gap (benchmark vs. deployment performance) does not narrow, then  G  is performative rather than effective.
5. Elasticity falsifier: If estimated elasticities  \delta, \epsilon, \zeta, \eta  are not significantly different from zero in the translog regression, then the corrective variables do not predict fragility reduction.
6. Identification falsifier: If the parameters cannot be identified from the available data with reasonable confidence intervals, the operational model is not empirically testable at the proposed scale.

A theory that cannot fail is not yet a scientific theory.

