Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Derivation of the Coupling Theorem from the Beta-Factor Model

Definition A.1.1 (Beta-factor model). Consider a redundant system with  n  nominally independent channels. Let  \lambda_{\text{total}}  be the total failure rate per channel per unit time. The beta-factor model decomposes this into:

\lambda_{\text{total}} = \lambda_{\text{independent}} + \lambda_{\text{common}}

where:

·  \lambda_{\text{independent}} = (1-\beta)\lambda_{\text{total}}  is the rate of failures affecting a single channel,
·  \lambda_{\text{common}} = \beta\lambda_{\text{total}}  is the rate of failures affecting all channels simultaneously,
·  \beta \in [0,1]  is the common-cause failure fraction (beta factor).

Definition A.1.2 (System failure probability). For a system requiring all  n  channels to function (a series system in reliability terms), system failure occurs if any channel fails. For a system requiring at least one channel to function (a parallel system), system failure occurs only if all channels fail.

The CLCA redundancy model treats the multiplanetary system as a parallel system: survival requires at least one node to remain functional. The probability that all  n  channels fail in a given interval  \Delta t  is:

P_{\text{fail}} = \underbrace{\beta \lambda_{\text{total}} \Delta t}_{\text{common-cause}} + \underbrace{(1-\beta)^n (\lambda_{\text{total}} \Delta t)^n}_{\text{independent, all channels}}

To leading order in  \Delta t :

P_{\text{fail}} \approx \beta + (1-\beta)^n p^n

where  p = \lambda_{\text{total}} \Delta t  is the per-channel failure probability.

Derivation A.1.3 (Effective redundancy). Define effective redundancy  R_{\text{eff}}  as the number of independent channels that would produce the same system failure probability as the actual coupled system. That is,  R_{\text{eff}}  satisfies:

\beta + (1-\beta)^{R_{\text{eff}}} p^{R_{\text{eff}}} = \beta + (1-\beta)^n p^n

This equation does not have a simple closed-form solution for general  n . However, in the regime of interest — where  p \ll 1  and redundancy is meant to provide protection — the independent failure term  (1-\beta)^n p^n  is negligible compared to  \beta  when  \beta > 0 . In this regime:

P_{\text{fail}} \approx \beta

and the system failure probability is dominated by common-cause failures. The effective redundancy is then defined operationally as the ratio of the independent-failure contribution to the total failure probability:

R_{\text{eff}} = \frac{(1-\beta)^n p^n}{\beta + (1-\beta)^n p^n} \cdot n

This is not a clean formula. To obtain a tractable form, we take a different approach based on failure rate attenuation.

Alternative derivation A.1.4 (Rate-based effective redundancy). Let the system-level failure rate be  \Lambda_{\text{sys}} . For a parallel system with  n  independent channels:

\Lambda_{\text{sys}}^{\text{indep}} = n \lambda_{\text{independent}} \cdot p^{n-1}

For a system with common-cause coupling:

\Lambda_{\text{sys}}^{\text{coupled}} = \lambda_{\text{common}} + n \lambda_{\text{independent}} \cdot p^{n-1}

Define  R_{\text{eff}}  as the number of independent channels that would yield the same system failure rate as the coupled system:

R_{\text{eff}} \cdot \lambda_{\text{independent}} \cdot p^{R_{\text{eff}}-1} = \lambda_{\text{common}} + n \lambda_{\text{independent}} \cdot p^{n-1}

For the common-cause term to dominate, we need  \lambda_{\text{common}} \gg n \lambda_{\text{independent}} p^{n-1} , which holds when  \beta  is not small and  p  is small. In this regime:

R_{\text{eff}} \approx \frac{\lambda_{\text{common}}}{\lambda_{\text{independent}} \cdot p^{R_{\text{eff}}-1}}

This is implicit. A more tractable formulation defines effective redundancy as:

\boxed{R_{\text{eff}} = \frac{R}{1 + \lambda K}}

where  R = n ,  K \in [0,1]  is a normalized coupling measure, and  \lambda  is derived as follows.

Derivation A.1.5 (Derivation of  \lambda ). Identify the coupling measure  K  with the common-cause failure fraction  \beta :

K = \beta

This identification is justified when the coupling is caused by shared substrate: the fraction of critical functions dependent on a shared substrate is the fraction of failures attributable to that substrate, which is precisely the beta factor.

The effective redundancy is then:

R_{\text{eff}} = \frac{R}{1 + \lambda \beta}

To determine  \lambda , impose the boundary conditions:

1. When  \beta = 0  (no common-cause coupling),  R_{\text{eff}} = R . This requires  \lambda \cdot 0 = 0 , which holds for any finite  \lambda .
2. When  \beta = 1  (complete common-cause coupling),  R_{\text{eff}} = 1 . That is, all redundancy is eliminated; the system behaves as a single channel. This requires:

\frac{R}{1 + \lambda \cdot 1} = 1 \implies \lambda = R - 1

But this makes  \lambda  depend on  R , which is undesirable:  \lambda  should be a property of the coupling, not the redundancy count.

3. A more natural boundary condition: when  \beta = 1 , the effective redundancy ratio  R_{\text{eff}}/R  should equal the independent failure fraction  1-\beta = 0 . That is,  R_{\text{eff}}/R \to 0  as  \beta \to 1 .

Using the form  R_{\text{eff}}/R = 1/(1+\lambda\beta) , we have:

\lim_{\beta \to 1} \frac{1}{1+\lambda\beta} = \frac{1}{1+\lambda}

For this to equal 0, we need  \lambda \to \infty . This is not a useful limit.

4. The correct boundary condition comes from the beta-factor model directly. The system failure probability for the coupled system is:

P_{\text{fail}} = \beta + (1-\beta)p^n

For the effective system with  R_{\text{eff}}  independent channels:

P_{\text{fail}}^{\text{eff}} = p^{R_{\text{eff}}}

Equating these:

p^{R_{\text{eff}}} = \beta + (1-\beta)p^n

Taking logarithms:

R_{\text{eff}} \ln p = \ln[\beta + (1-\beta)p^n]

For  p \ll 1  and  \beta > 0 :

R_{\text{eff}} \approx \frac{\ln \beta}{\ln p}

This is negative (since  \ln \beta < 0  and  \ln p < 0 ), which is meaningless. The issue is that the effective redundancy framework breaks down when common-cause failure dominates: there is no number of independent channels that reproduces a common-cause failure probability.

Resolution A.1.6. The effective redundancy formula  R_{\text{eff}} = R/(1+\lambda K)  should be understood as a phenomenological interpolation between two regimes:

·  K \approx 0 :  R_{\text{eff}} \approx R  (redundancy provides full benefit)
·  K \approx 1 :  R_{\text{eff}} \approx R/(1+\lambda)  (redundancy provides attenuated benefit)

The parameter  \lambda  is the coupling sensitivity, estimated from data rather than derived from first principles. The beta-factor model provides the motivation for the functional form (effective redundancy decreases with coupling) but not the exact parameterization.

Proposition A.1.7 (Coupling condition). If  R_{\text{eff}} = R/(1+\lambda K) , then:

\frac{d\ln R_{\text{eff}}}{dt} = \frac{d\ln R}{dt} - \frac{\lambda K}{1+\lambda K} \cdot \frac{d\ln K}{dt}

Proof. Taking logarithms:

\ln R_{\text{eff}} = \ln R - \ln(1+\lambda K)

Differentiating with respect to time:

\frac{d\ln R_{\text{eff}}}{dt} = \frac{d\ln R}{dt} - \frac{\lambda}{1+\lambda K} \cdot \frac{dK}{dt}

Since  dK/dt = K \cdot d\ln K/dt :

\frac{d\ln R_{\text{eff}}}{dt} = \frac{d\ln R}{dt} - \frac{\lambda K}{1+\lambda K} \cdot \frac{d\ln K}{dt}

Therefore,  d\ln R_{\text{eff}}/dt < 0  if and only if:

\frac{d\ln K}{dt} > \frac{1+\lambda K}{\lambda K} \cdot \frac{d\ln R}{dt}

A sufficient condition is:

\frac{d\ln K}{dt} > \frac{d\ln R}{dt}

when  \lambda K > 0 . This is the condition stated in the main text.  \square 

Corollary A.1.8. If  K  grows exponentially at rate  g_K  and  R  grows exponentially at rate  g_R , then  R_{\text{eff}}  declines if  g_K > g_R , regardless of the value of  \lambda .

