A.3 Translog Model: Properties and Nested Tests

Definition A.3.1 (Translog fragility function). Let  \mathbf{X} = (X_1, \ldots, X_m)  be the vector of log-transformed variables:

\mathbf{X} = (\ln P, \ln A, \ln D, \ln G_{\text{eff}}, \ln V_{\text{eff}}, \ln C_{\text{eff}}, \ln R_{\text{eff}}, \ln K)

The translog fragility function is:

\ln F = \alpha_0 + \sum_{i=1}^m \alpha_i X_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^m \sum_{j=1}^m \beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \varepsilon

with symmetry  \beta_{ij} = \beta_{ji}  for all  i,j .

Proposition A.3.2 (Nested tests). The translog model nests the log-linear model. The log-linear model is obtained by imposing  \beta_{ij} = 0  for all  i,j . The hypothesis of no interaction can be tested via a standard F-test or likelihood ratio test with  m(m+1)/2  restrictions.

Proof. Setting  \beta_{ij} = 0  reduces the translog to  \ln F = \alpha_0 + \sum_i \alpha_i X_i + \varepsilon , which is the log-linear form.  \square 

Proposition A.3.3 (Elasticity interpretation). In the translog model, the elasticity of  F  with respect to  X_i  is:

\frac{\partial \ln F}{\partial X_i} = \alpha_i + \sum_{j=1}^m \beta_{ij} X_j

This is not constant: it depends on the levels of all other variables. This is the key advantage over the log-linear model, where the elasticity is  \alpha_i  regardless of context.

Corollary A.3.4. The hypothesis that governance is more effective when strategic divergence is low corresponds to  \beta_{G,D} < 0 . The hypothesis that coupling attenuates the benefit of redundancy corresponds to  \beta_{K,R} < 0 . These are testable hypotheses.

---

A.4 Barrier Function: Properties and Limiting Cases

Definition A.4.1 (Barrier function). The barrier function  h(E)  satisfies:

1.  h(E) \geq 1  for all  E 
2.  \partial h/\partial E_i > 0  for ecological variables whose increase raises fragility
3.  h(E) \to \infty  as  E_i \to E_i^{\text{boundary}}  for at least one  i 

Proposition A.4.2 (Candidate functional forms).

Form 1 (Power-law barrier):

h(E) = \prod_i \left(1 + \gamma_i \left(\frac{E_i}{E_i^{\text{boundary}}}\right)^{\theta_i}\right)

As  E_i \to E_i^{\text{boundary}} ,  h \to \prod_{j \neq i} (\ldots) \cdot (1+\gamma_i) , which is finite. This form does not satisfy condition 3 unless  \theta_i \to \infty .

Form 2 (Rational barrier):

h(E) = \prod_i \frac{1}{1 - (E_i/E_i^{\text{boundary}})^{\theta_i}}

As  E_i \to E_i^{\text{boundary}} ,  h \to \infty . This satisfies condition 3.

Form 3 (Exponential barrier):

h(E) = \prod_i \exp\left(\gamma_i \frac{E_i}{E_i^{\text{boundary}} - E_i}\right)

As  E_i \to E_i^{\text{boundary}} ,  h \to \infty . This also satisfies condition 3.

Proposition A.4.3 (Selection among forms). The choice among barrier function forms is an empirical question. Form 2 is the most tractable for estimation because it is log-linear in the transformed variable  \ln(1 - (E_i/E_i^{\text{boundary}})^{\theta_i}) . Form 3 is more flexible but has a singularity that may cause numerical issues.

Recommendation A.4.4. Use Form 2 with  \theta_i = 1  as the baseline specification, and test  \theta_i \neq 1  as a robustness check. The baseline form is:

h(E) = \prod_i \frac{1}{1 - E_i/E_i^{\text{boundary}}}

---

A.5 Delay Kernel: Stability Analysis

Definition A.5.1 (Delay differential equation). The operational model with delays can be written as:

\frac{d\ln F}{dt} = f(\ln F(t), \ln P(t), \ldots, \ln G(t-\tau_G), \ldots)

This is a delay differential equation (DDE). The stability of the system depends on the delays  \tau_G, \tau_V, \tau_C, \tau_R .

Proposition A.5.2 (Stability condition). For a linear DDE of the form:

\frac{dx}{dt} = -a x(t) + b x(t-\tau)

the system is stable if and only if:

a > b \cos(\omega \tau) \quad \text{for all } \omega \text{ satisfying } \omega = -b \sin(\omega \tau)

A sufficient condition for stability is  a > b . A necessary condition is  a > -b .

Corollary A.5.3. In the CLCA context, the "correction" terms ( G, V, C, R ) act as negative feedback with delay. The system is stable — fragility declines without oscillation — if the corrective feedback is sufficiently strong relative to the delay. If the delay is too long relative to the feedback strength, the system can oscillate: fragility may decline, then increase, then decline again.

Implication A.5.4. The delays  \tau_G, \tau_V, \tau_C, \tau_R  are not merely empirical parameters; they affect the qualitative behavior of the system. A civilization with strong corrective capacity but long delays may exhibit oscillatory fragility dynamics. This is a testable prediction: historical time series of fragility should show oscillations if delays are long.

